<ToolLayout title="Common Software Design Patterns Reference" description="Quick reference for Singleton, Factory, Observer, Strategy, Decorator, Repository, and Adapter patterns.">
  <DesignPatternsReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why patterns are a shared vocabulary, not rules</h2>
  <p>Design patterns aren't mandatory structures to force into every project — they're named solutions to recurring problems, useful mainly because naming them lets developers communicate design intent quickly ("just make it a Strategy") instead of re-explaining the same structure from scratch each time.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the structural problem you're facing (object creation, notification, interchangeable behavior, etc).</li>
    <li>Check if a named pattern already solves it cleanly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Design Pattern Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forcing a pattern where simpler code would do.</strong> Overusing patterns (especially Singleton) can add indirection without real benefit — not every problem needs a named pattern applied.</li>
    <li><strong>Using Singleton for global state</strong> that would be better handled with explicit dependency injection, since Singletons can make testing and reasoning about state harder.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing Strategy and Factory</strong> — Strategy is about interchangeable behavior at runtime, Factory is about how objects get created in the first place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Reference Design Patterns</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Communicating a design approach concisely during a code review or design discussion</li>
    <li>Recognizing an existing pattern already in use in unfamiliar code</li>
    <li>Choosing between a few structural approaches when designing a new component</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Software Design Patterns FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Do I need to memorize all of these?</strong> No — recognizing the problem each pattern solves is more valuable than memorizing exact implementations; you can look up specifics when the situation calls for it.</p>
</ToolLayout>
